WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.008
Retrieval metrics: the half you can measure exactly.

2
00:00:03.288 --> 00:00:07.960
Retrieval metrics need one label per question: which chunks contain the answer.

3
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.949
With that, Studio 2’s metrics apply directly.

4
00:00:11.229 --> 00:00:15.709
Recall@k is the fraction of relevant chunks that appear in the top k.

5
00:00:15.989 --> 00:00:19.637
Precision@k is the fraction of the top k that are relevant.

6
00:00:19.917 --> 00:00:25.037
MRR (mean reciprocal rank) rewards putting the first relevant chunk near the top.

7
00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:29.648
All three are deterministic code: no model, no judge, no drift.

8
00:00:29.928 --> 00:00:32.019
That is a strong reason to lean on them.

9
00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:34.709
For RAG, recall usually matters most.

10
00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:41.368
A generator can often ignore an irrelevant passage, but it cannot use a passage that was never retrieved.

11
00:00:41.648 --> 00:00:50.459
Precision still matters for cost, latency, and distraction, since every extra chunk is tokens you pay for and noise the model must ignore.

12
00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:57.203
Report both at the k your system actually uses, plus a smaller k if you are considering a reranker.

13
00:00:57.483 --> 00:00:58.891
Where do labels come from?

14
00:00:59.171 --> 00:01:01.027
Two sources, and you want both.

15
00:01:01.307 --> 00:01:05.957
Real questions from logs or users, labeled by someone who knows the corpus.

16
00:01:06.237 --> 00:01:17.715
Synthetic questions: the FAQ suggests taking a document, extracting its key facts, and generating questions those facts answer, which gives you a label for free (the source chunk).

17
00:01:17.995 --> 00:01:27.680
Synthetic questions are cheap and plentiful but tend to reuse the document’s wording, which flatters keyword search and makes retrieval look easier than it is.

18
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.227
Check a sample by hand, and report real and synthetic results separately.

19
00:01:32.507 --> 00:01:33.445
Two traps.

20
00:01:33.725 --> 00:01:40.595
A question whose answer is spread across several chunks needs all of them labeled, or recall will look worse than it is.

21
00:01:40.875 --> 00:01:45.973
And if your chunking changes, chunk ids change, so labels keyed to ids break.

22
00:01:46.253 --> 00:01:50.925
Key labels to stable document positions or to the answer text where you can.
